  "body": {
    "id": "thm:finite-sample-coverage",
    "kind": "theorem",
    "statement": "Under independent multinomial sampling, for every \\(n\\ge1\\), \\(\\inf_{P\\in\\mathcal M_{\\eta,k_0}^{1\\mathrm c}}P^n\\{\\mathcal I_{k_0}^{1\\mathrm c}(P)\\subseteq\\mathcal C_{n,k_0}^{\\tau}\\}\\ge1-\\alpha\\). The event covers both marginal identified sets simultaneously. No endpoint-consistency, Hausdorff-consistency, or branch-persistence conclusion is asserted.",
    "free_symbols": [
      "P",
      "M_one",
      "eta",
      "k0",
      "I_one",
      "C_tau",
      "alpha",
      "W_n",
      "n"
    ],
    "depends_on": [
      "ass:iid-sampling",
      "def:confidence-inversion",
      "def:one-collision-region",
      "thm:exact-one-collision-region"
    ],
    "justification": "This auxiliary appendix-tier theorem covers the entire disconnected identified set uniformly at every sample size by inverting the full empirical PMF.",
    "gap": "Finite-sample outer coverage alone does not imply branch persistence or set-estimation consistency; those conclusions require the separate explicit continuity conditions.",
    "consumer": "Applied analyses obtain honest simultaneous coverage of both marginal sets without selecting one latent alignment.",
    "proof_tex": "Fix a cell \\(w\\) and put \\(Y_r=\\mathbf 1\\{W_r=w\\}\\) and \\(q=P(w)\\).  Under independent multinomial sampling, the \\(Y_r\\)'s are independent Bernoulli variables of mean \\(q\\).  For every real \\(\\lambda\\),\n\\[\n \\mathbb E\\exp\\{\\lambda(Y_r-q)\\}\n =(1-q)e^{-\\lambda q}+qe^{\\lambda(1-q)}\n \\leq e^{\\lambda^2/8}.\n \\tag{17}\n\\]\nIndeed, if \\(K\\) is the logarithm of the middle expression, exponential tilting remains Bernoulli, so \\(K(0)=K'(0)=0\\) and \\(K''(\\lambda)=q_\\lambda(1-q_\\lambda)\\leq1/4\\).  Taylor's formula with integral remainder proves (17).\n\nFor any nonnegative \\(Z\\) and \\(a>0\\), \\(\\mathbf 1\\{Z\\geq a\\}\\leq Z/a\\), so taking expectations gives \\(P\\{Z\\geq a\\}\\leq \\mathbb E[Z]/a\\).  Apply this inequality to the exponential of the centered sum.  Independence and (17), for \\(\\lambda>0\\), give\n\\[\n P^n\\{\\widehat P_n(w)-P(w)\\geq\\delta\\}\n \\leq\\exp\\{-\\lambda n\\delta+n\\lambda^2/8\\}.\n\\]\nChoosing \\(\\lambda=4\\delta\\) and applying the same calculation to \\(-Y_r\\) gives\n\\[\n P^n\\{|\\widehat P_n(w)-P(w)|>\\delta\\}\\leq2e^{-2n\\delta^2}.\n \\tag{18}\n\\]\nThere are thirty-two observed cells.  The union bound and the stated value of \\(\\delta_n(\\alpha)\\) yield, uniformly over all multinomial laws,\n\\[\n P^n\\{\\|\\widehat P_n-P\\|_\\infty>\\delta_n(\\alpha)\\}\n \\leq64\\exp\\{-2n\\delta_n(\\alpha)^2\\}=\\alpha.\n \\tag{19}\n\\]\nOn the complementary event, the true \\(P\\), which by hypothesis belongs to \\(\\mathcal M_{\\eta,k_0}^{1\\mathrm c}\\), is one of the laws \\(Q\\) in the union defining \\(\\mathcal C_{n,k_0}^{\\tau}\\).  Therefore the whole sharp set \\(\\mathcal I_{k_0}^{1\\mathrm c}(P)\\), rather than a selected latent value, is contained in \\(\\mathcal C_{n,k_0}^{\\tau}\\).  Taking probabilities in (19) and then the infimum over \\(P\\in\\mathcal M_{\\eta,k_0}^{1\\mathrm c}\\) proves\n\\[\n \\inf_{P\\in\\mathcal M_{\\eta,k_0}^{1\\mathrm c}}\n P^n\\{\\mathcal I_{k_0}^{1\\mathrm c}(P)\\subseteq\\mathcal C_{n,k_0}^{\\tau}\\}\n \\geq1-\\alpha.\n\\]\nProjection of this one joint containment event covers both marginal identified sets simultaneously.  The radius tends to zero, but fixed-coverage containment alone supplies neither lower hemicontinuity nor branch persistence; hence it does not by itself imply the endpoint or Hausdorff conclusions excluded by the theorem.",
